  "text": "umably, the question is asking for the largest value of \\( k \\) for which the cubic polynomial \\( P(x) \\) has three distinct real solutions. This means that \\( P(x) \\) should cross the x-axis at three distinct points, i.e., it should have three real roots.\n\nLet's denote the cubic polynomial as \\( P(x) = ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d \\).\n\nGiven:\n1. \\( P(0) = k \\) ⟹ \\( d = k \\)\n2. \\( P(1) = 2k \\) ⟹ \\( a + b + c + d = 2k \\)\n3. \\( P(-1) = 3k \\) ⟹ \\( -a + b - c + d = 3k \\)\n\nSubstituting \\( d = k \\) into the equations:\n1. \\( a + b + c + k = 2k \\) ⟹ \\( a + b + c = k \\)\n2. \\( -a + b - c + k = 3k \\) ⟹ \\( -a + b - c = 2k \\)\n\nAdding these two equations:\n\\[\n(a + b + c) + (-a + b - c) = k + 2k \\\\\n2b = 3k \\\\\nb = \\frac{3k}{2}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\( b = \\frac{3k}{2} \\) into the first equation:\n\\[\na + \\frac{3k}{2} + c = k \\\\\na + c = k - \\frac{3k}{2} \\\\\na + c = -\\frac{k}{2}\n\\]\n\nNow, let's consider the cubic polynomial:\n\\[\nP(x) = ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + k\n\\]\n\nFor \\( P(x) \\) to have three distinct real roots, the discriminant of the cubic equation must be positive. The discriminant \\( D \\) of a cubic polynomial \\( ax^3 + bx^2 + cx + d \\) is given by:\n\\[\nD = 18abcd - 4b^3d + b^2c^2 - 4ac^3 - 27a^2d^2\n\\]\n\nSubstituting \\( a = -\\frac{k}{2} \\), \\( b = \\frac{3k}{2} \\), \\( c = -\\frac{k}{2} \\), and \\( d = k \\):\n\\[\nD = 18 \\left(-\\frac{k}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{3k}{2}\\right) \\left(-\\frac{k}{2}\\right) k - 4 \\left(\\frac{3k}{2}\\right)^3 k + \\left(\\frac{3k}{2}\\right)^2 \\left(-\\frac{k}{2}\\right)^2 - 4 \\left(-\\frac{k}{2}\\right) \\left(-\\frac{k}{2}\\right)^3 - 27 \\left(-\\frac{k}{2}\\right)^2 k^2\n\\]\n\nSimplifying each term:\n\\[\nD = 18 \\left(-\\frac{k}{2}\\right) \\left(\\frac{3k}{2}\\right) \\left(-\\frac{k}{2}\\right) k - 4 \\left(\\frac{27k^4}{8}\\right) k + \\left(\\frac{9k^2}{4}\\right) \\left(\\frac{k^2}{4}\\right) - 4 \\left(\\frac{k^4}{8}\\right) - 27 \\left(\\frac{k^2}{4}\\right) k^2\n\\]\n\\[\nD = 18 \\left(\\frac{3k^4}{8}\\right) - \\frac{108k^5}{8} + \\frac{9k^4}{16} - \\frac{k^4}{2} - \\frac{27k^4}{4}\n\\]\n\\[\nD = \\frac{54k^4}{8} - \\frac{108k^5}{8} + \\frac{9k^4}{16} - \\frac{8k^4}{16} - \\frac{108k^4}{16}\n\\]\n\\[\nD = \\frac{54k^4}{8} - \\frac{108k^5}{8} + \\frac{9k^4 - 8k^4 - 108k^4}{16}\n\\]\n\\[\nD = \\frac{54k^4}{8} - \\frac{108k^5}{8} - \\frac{107k^4}{16}\n\\]\n\\[\nD = \\frac{108k^4 - 216k^5 - 107k^4}{16}\n\\]\n\\[\nD = \\frac{k^4 - 216k^5}{16}\n\\]\n\nFor \\( D > 0 \\):\n\\[\nk^4 - 216k^5 > 0 \\\\\nk^4(1 - 216k) > 0\n\\]\n\nSince \\( k^4 > 0 \\) for all \\( k \\neq 0 \\), the inequality \\( 1 - 216k > 0 \\) must hold:\n\\[\n1 - 216k > 0 \\\\\n216k < 1 \\\\\nk < \\frac{1}{216}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the largest value of \\( k \\) for which \\( P(x) \\) has three distinct real solutions is \\( \\frac{1}{216} \\).\n\n\\boxed{\\frac{1}{216}}",
